As your application grows, you'll want a database that handles scalability.

Your first option is vertical scaling - adding more CPU, RAM, or storage to your existing server. MongoDB supports this approach effectively, and with Atlas, you can automatically scale compute and storage resources to handle traffic spikes without manual intervention.

However, vertical scaling has economic and physical limits. As you start handling millions of users and massive datasets, the cost of upgrading hardware becomes prohibitive. This is where MongoDB's horizontal scaling capabilities become essential.

MongoDB handles horizontal scaling through sharding - partitioning data across multiple machines. Each shard typically runs as a replica set, providing both horizontal scale and fault tolerance.

The system distributes data across shards automatically based on your chosen shard key. Query routing is handled transparently by mongos, MongoDB's query router, so your application code remains unchanged.

Unlike solutions that require external tools and complex logic for horizontal scaling, MongoDB provides native sharding capabilities integrated into the database engine.

